- Retrieved practices:
- Heartbleed-style unchecked payload length in memory copy
WHAT: Code copies `payload` bytes from a packet buffer into an allocated buffer without verifying that `payload` is ≤ the actual received data length, causing out-of-bounds reads.
WHY: An attacker can request more bytes than provided, leaking arbitrary process memory (keys, credentials, sensitive data) over the network.
HOW: Always validate length fields from untrusted input against the actual buffer/record size before allocation and memcpy; reject or truncate when `payload > available_data_length`.
EXAMPLE:
Before (vulnerable): `buffer = malloc(1+2+payload+padding); memcpy(bp, pl, payload);`
After (safe): `if (payload > record_len - header_len) return error; buffer = malloc(1+2+payload+padding); memcpy(bp, pl, payload);`

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and null terminators
WHAT: Failing to allocate enough space for strings, including the terminating null byte, can cause buffer overflows when copying or manipulating strings.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, crash the program, or enable code execution vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always compute required size as `strlen(source) + 1` for the null terminator, allocate at least that many bytes, and use bounds-checking string functions that take the destination size (for C, e.g., `strcpy_s(dest, dest_size, src)`).
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `char *p = malloc(strlen(name)); strcpy(p, name);` After (safer): `size_t n = strlen(name) + 1; char *p = malloc(n); if (p && strcpy_s(p, n, name) == 0) { /* use p */ }`

- Null-termination when using strncpy and string APIs
WHAT: Passing a non-null-terminated character sequence to a library function that expects a C string (for example, strlen) causes undefined behavior, which can occur when strncpy does not write a terminating '\0'.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to information disclosure, crashes, or exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities.
HOW: When copying into fixed-size buffers with strncpy, copy at most size-1 bytes and explicitly write a '\0' terminator; never call string functions on buffers that are not guaranteed to be null-terminated.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant, may lack terminator): `strncpy(c_str, source, sizeof(c_str)); ret = strlen(c_str);` After (compliant): `strncpy(c_str, source, sizeof(c_str) - 1); c_str[sizeof(c_str) - 1] = '\0'; ret = strlen(c_str);`

- Do not pass a non-null-terminated character sequence to a library function that expects a string
WHAT: Passing a character buffer that is not guaranteed to be null-terminated to string-handling library functions (e.g., strlen, strcpy) causes reads past the buffer end.
WHY: This can lead to buffer over-reads, information disclosure, crashes, or further memory corruption.
HOW: Always ensure destination buffers are large enough, explicitly write a terminating '\0', and validate string length before calling string functions; use safer bounded APIs (e.g., strncpy, strnlen) and enforce null termination after copying.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `strcpy(dst, src); ret = strlen(dst);` After (safe): `strncpy(dst, src, sizeof(dst)-1); dst[sizeof(dst)-1] = '\0'; ret = strnlen(dst, sizeof(dst));`

- Do not access freed memory
WHAT: Accessing memory after it has been freed (including using stale pointers after realloc) can lead to use-after-free or double-free conditions.
WHY: Use-after-free and double-free bugs can cause crashes, data corruption, or be exploited for arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Immediately update all pointers after realloc, set freed pointers to NULL, and ensure no code path uses a pointer after free; avoid freeing the same allocation more than once.
EXAMPLE (C):
// Before: stale pointer after realloc
p = realloc(p, new_size); use_old_alias(p_alias); // p_alias still points to old memory
// After: update all aliases and nullify on free
tmp = realloc(p, new_size); if (!tmp) handle_error(); p = tmp; p_alias = p; free(p); p = p_alias = NULL;

- MEM31-C: Free dynamically allocated memory when no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to call free() on dynamically allocated memory (e.g., from malloc()) before the lifetime of the last pointer to it ends causes memory to remain permanently allocated (memory leak).
WHY: Memory leaks can exhaust available memory, leading to degraded performance, denial of service, or process termination over time.
HOW: For every successful call to a standard allocation function (malloc, calloc, realloc), ensure a corresponding free() is invoked on the same pointer before all references to it go out of scope or are overwritten; structure code so every exit path (including error paths) frees owned allocations.
EXAMPLE:
Noncompliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; return 0;`
Compliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; /* use buf */ free(buf); return 0;`



